1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.910
This video demonstrates how to perform

2
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:11.580
a front-of-the-eye examination using the anterior

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.957
second loop of the Arclight device.

4
00:00:14.957 --> 00:00:18.897
Start with hand hygiene and introduce yourself.

5
00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:19.460
Confirm

6
00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:25.305
the identity of the patient, explain the examination and gain consent to perform it.

7
00:00:25.305 --> 00:00:26.140
Initially take

8
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.841
a few seconds to observe both eyes at the same time.

9
00:00:29.841 --> 00:00:31.860
Look at the periorbitor, eyelids and

10
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.540
eyes, commenting on any swelling, redness and discharge, as well as prominence of the eyes

11
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.694
or abnormal eyelid position.

12
00:00:39.694 --> 00:00:44.540
Then perform a magnified examination using the arc light.

13
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.966
Switch on the white light and hold the device close to your eye.

14
00:00:47.966 --> 00:00:49.020
Slowly approach the patient's

15
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.277
right eye first until it is in focus.

16
00:00:51.277 --> 00:00:53.252
This will be at around 6 cm.

17
00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:54.380
Ask the patient to

18
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:58.940
move their eye outwards and inwards, then up and down, while gently holding their eyelids,

19
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.442
systematically noting a different structure scene, thinking of them in groups.

20
00:01:03.442 --> 00:01:04.260
The first

21
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:11.360
This group includes the upper and lower eyelashes and lid margins, the second includes the conjunctiva

22
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.220
and sclera, and the lower forenax.

23
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.552
The third group is the cornea, the junction of this with a sclera called

24
00:01:20.552 --> 00:01:21.480
the limbis, and

25
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.380
the pupil, surrounded by the iris.

26
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.940
Once you have finished noting all these structures, move on to assessing the anterior chamber depth.

27
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.100
The anterior chamber is the space between the cornea and the iris.

28
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.514
By shining the light from the temporal side, the depth of the anterior chamber

29
00:01:38.514 --> 00:01:39.460
can be assessed.

30
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.220
A shadow on the nasal iris can suggest a shallow anterior chamber.

31
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.380
This is a normal examination with no shadow.

32
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.020
Now select the blue light on the arc light.

33
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.140
With fluorescing dye the tear film, areas of epithelial loss and disease will be highlighted.

34
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.140
The dye fluoresces brightly with the blue light.

35
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.200
a diagram the site and size of areas of epithelial loss and disease.

36
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.300
Finished by examining the superior tarsal plate, place a cotton bud on the upper lid,

37
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.400
then gently lift the lashes upwards while slowly pressing downwards with the bud.

38
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.920
Initially observed with a naked eye, then bring in the arc light for a magnified view.

39
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.180
Repeat this examination on the other eye.

40
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.360
Afterwards, thank the patient, summarizing the records of findings and repeat hand hygiene.

41
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.360
Please have a look at our other University of St Andrews Arc Light project videos.
